The key insight in this rule is to explicitly decide whether sending a robot to maintenance now is beneficial: if a robot has only one duty left before maintenance, we check whether it can finish maintenance and return before the horizon ends. If it can, we flush it early (prioritizing those with longer maintenance times so they finish in time); if it cannot, we save it as a backup and prefer other robots. This avoids unnecessary maintenance events near the end of the horizon, which reduces both the number of robots opened and the imbalance in first-maintenance times. The rule also interleaves a flush-oriented order with a load-balancing order for both unmaintained and maintained robots, giving the genome a diverse set of reuse alternatives while keeping the default choice strong. A representability check ensures the remaining horizon can still be exactly covered by the available duty lengths.

